course: biology
lesson: 161
title: Borneo: orangutans, proboscis monkeys, the biggest flower and gliding snakes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Borneo is a huge island in Southeast Asia covered in some of the oldest rainforest on Earth, where trees grow taller than a thirty-storey building.
explain: Every evening an orangutan climbs high, bends branches into a springy platform and piles leaves on top, a bit like you making a den out of cushions, and the next night it builds a brand-new bed.
explain: The forest is full of oddities: a monkey with a nose like a dangling cucumber, a flower as wide as a car tyre that stinks of rotting meat, and snakes, frogs and lizards that glide between the trees.
explain: Many of these animals and plants live nowhere else, so when the forest is cut down they have nowhere to go.
fact: Orangutans move about the forest to find food and make a fresh nest every night wherever they end up, by bending and weaving branches, and they sometimes hold big leaves over their heads like umbrellas when it rains.
fact: Male proboscis monkeys have long dangling noses that make their calls louder, and proboscis monkeys swim well with partly webbed feet.
fact: Rafflesia arnoldii has the biggest single flower in the world, up to about a metre across, and it smells of rotting meat to attract flies that carry its pollen.
fact: Rafflesia has no leaves, stems or roots of its own and lives inside a vine as a parasite.
fact: Pitcher plants trap insects that slip down the waxy walls into the liquid, where they are digested; one Borneo pitcher plant works as a toilet for tree shrews, which lick nectar from its lid and leave droppings the plant feeds on.
fact: Paradise tree snakes flatten their bodies and wriggle in the air to glide from tree to tree; Borneo also has gliding frogs, lizards and squirrels, perhaps because its tall trees stand far apart.
fact: Borneo has some of the smallest frogs in the world, smaller than a pea, and one tiny kind lays its eggs in pitcher plants.
fact: The moonrat smells strongly of garlic or onion, and despite its name it is not a rat but a cousin of the hedgehog.
fact: The clouded leopard has flexible ankles and a long tail, so it can climb down trees head first and hang upside down; it has the longest canine teeth for its size of any living cat.
fact: The female rhinoceros hornbill seals herself into a tree hole with mud and food leftovers and stays inside for weeks, safe from snakes and monkeys, while the male passes food through a slit.
fact: Bornean elephants are smaller than other Asian elephants, with bigger ears and long tails; some scientists think people brought them long ago, others think they have lived there for thousands of years.
fact: Borneo's forest trees are hung with giant ferns and other plants that grow on branches, and some of its trees are over ninety metres tall.
exercise: How often does an orangutan make a new bed? | Every night | Only once a year | Only when it rains | Never, it sleeps on the ground
exercise: What does an orangutan sometimes do with a big leaf in the rain? | Holds it over its head like an umbrella | Eats it to stay warm | Wraps its baby in it like a nappy for the night | Throws it at other orangutans
exercise: What does a male proboscis monkey's huge nose help with? | Making its calls louder | Smelling fruit from far away | Breathing under water | Digging for roots
exercise: Which skill are proboscis monkeys good at? | Swimming | Gliding between trees | Digging burrows | Running on sand
exercise: How wide can a Rafflesia flower be? | About a metre, as wide as a car tyre | About the size of a coin | About ten metres, as wide as a house | About the size of a dinner plate
exercise: Why does Rafflesia smell of rotting meat? | To attract flies that carry its pollen | To scare away elephants | To warn people it is poisonous | Because it catches and eats dead animals
exercise: How does Rafflesia get its food? | It lives inside a vine as a parasite | It makes food in its big green leaves | It catches insects in a pitcher | It sucks water up through long roots
exercise: How does a pitcher plant catch insects? | They slip down waxy walls into a liquid that digests them | It grabs them with a snapping lid, like a mouth with teeth | It shoots sticky threads at them | It stings them with tiny hairs
exercise: What do tree shrews give one Borneo pitcher plant? | Their droppings, which the plant feeds on | Their warm fur, which keeps the plant from freezing | Their babies, which it protects | Seeds, which it plants
exercise: How does a paradise tree snake glide? | It flattens its body and wriggles in the air | It flaps skin wings like a bat | It holds a leaf like a parachute | It jumps from the back of a big bird in mid-flight
exercise: Where does one tiny Borneo frog lay its eggs? | In pitcher plants | Out in the salty sea | Inside fallen coconuts | In a hornbill's nest
exercise: What is the moonrat? | A hedgehog cousin that smells of garlic | A rat that only comes out at full moon | A kind of bat | A small monkey
exercise: What can a clouded leopard do that most cats cannot? | Climb down a tree head first | Swim under water for a whole hour | Run at a hundred kilometres an hour | Change colour like a chameleon
exercise: Why does the female rhinoceros hornbill seal herself in a tree hole? | To keep her and her chick safe from snakes and monkeys | Because she is too heavy to fly and needs a safe place | To hide from the rain | To sleep through the winter
exercise: How are Bornean elephants different from other Asian elephants? | They are smaller with bigger ears and long tails | They are bigger with tiny ears | They have no trunks | They are covered in long hair, like the mammoths were
exercise: Do scientists agree how elephants got to Borneo? | No, some think people brought them and some think they were always there | Yes, they all agree elephants swam there last year | Yes, they all agree elephants came from Africa | No elephants live in Borneo
exercise: How tall can some Borneo forest trees grow? | Over ninety metres | About two metres | About ten metres | Over a kilometre
test: «my cousin says rafflesia smells lovely like roses» What is right? | No, it smells of rotting meat to attract flies | Yes, it is the sweetest-smelling flower in the world, like roses | Yes, bees love its smell | No, it has no smell at all
test: «saw 3 orangutans in the zoo vid, how many arms is that lol» | 6 | 3 | 12 | 9
test: «moonrat, so it's a rat innit» What is right? | No, it is a cousin of the hedgehog | Yes, the biggest rat in Asia | Yes, a rat that lives on the moon | No, it is a kind of monkey
test: In a cartoon a snake flies by flapping like a bird. What is real for Borneo's snakes? | They glide by flattening their bodies, they cannot flap | They really do flap like birds | No snake ever leaves the ground | They glide on big leaves, using them like surfboards
test: What would happen if a hornbill mum's tree hole had no mud wall? | Snakes and monkeys could reach her and her chick | She would get too hot | She could not lay any eggs | Nothing at all, because the mud is only there as decoration
test: A girl finds a big flower with no leaves and no stem growing out of a vine. What is most likely true? | It is a parasite getting its food from the vine | It makes all its own food from sunlight in its big red petals | It has fallen off a tree | It is a fake flower made of plastic
test: «why do they need a new bed every night, lazy to clean or what» Which reason fits best? | They move around to find food, so they sleep where they end up | They are scared of their old beds | Their beds always fall down by the morning, so they need new ones | They eat their beds for breakfast
test: «2 proboscis monkeys n a clouded leopard in a tree, how many tails» | 3 | 2 | 1 | 6
test: Which animal would be best at climbing down a tree trunk head first? | A clouded leopard | A proboscis monkey's baby | A Bornean pygmy elephant | A moonrat on the forest floor
test: «bloody hell that pitcher's full of dead flies, what's it doing» What is right? | Digesting them for food | Saving them for birds to eat later | Using them as decoration | Nothing, they just fell in by chance
test: If Borneo's forest were cleared, what would happen to animals like orangutans that live nowhere else? | They would have nowhere else to go and could die out | They would move to the Arctic | Nothing, they live in cities too | They would turn into new kinds of animals at once and live on the ground
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the nutrition of Rafflesia arnoldii. | It derives its nourishment as a parasite within a host vine | It photosynthesises by means of extensive foliage | It captures insects within a fluid-filled pitcher | It absorbs nutrients from the soil through an extensive root system of its own
exam: Identify the principal function attributed to the enlarged nose of the male proboscis monkey. | The amplification of its vocal calls | The detection of fruit at great distances | The facilitation of breathing whilst submerged | The excavation of edible roots
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the nesting behaviour of the orangutan? | A fresh nest of woven branches is constructed every night | A single nest is occupied throughout the whole of life | Nests are constructed upon the ground beneath the trees | No nest is constructed under any circumstances
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the female rhinoceros hornbill encloses herself within a tree cavity. | Protection of herself and her offspring from predators such as snakes and monkeys | Avoidance of heavy rainfall during the monsoon | Escape from the heat of the midday sun | Concealment from the male of her species
